<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!--  Chapter 3: Exploring Data                                         -->
<!--                                                                    -->
<!--  Data-first chapter. Deliberately light: notice patterns, ask      -->
<!--  questions, read a pivot table. No formal definitions of mean,     -->
<!--  variance, missingness taxonomy, etc. - those are introduced only  -->
<!--  when they are needed, in later chapters:                          -->
<!--  This chapter's job is only to make the reader curious enough to   -->
<!--  want those later chapters.                                        -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-exploring-data" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exploring Data</title>

  <introduction xml:id="intro-exploring-data">
    <p>
      This chapter is all about exploring data and noticing patterns.
      It starts with a case study on road safety in Kenya, using a paper
      analysing data from the National Transport and Safety Authority (NTSA).
      Then, we will look at how to visualise and summarise this data to uncover
      patterns and insights.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 0, DRAFT A (ACTIVE): why explore at all - kenya crash data -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-malaria-map-hook">
    <title>Why Explore At All?</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let's start with something that affects all of us: road safety.
        Since 2012, the National Transport and Safety Authority (NTSA) has been
        recording road crash data from across the country, such as the county,
        the road, the time, the type of vehicle, and who was involved.
        They publish summaries of this on their website.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-crashes">
      <title>Who is dying on Kenya's roads, and where?</title>

      <p>
        The NTSA publish two kinds of records.
        The first is a daily comparative report.
        This gives the number of people killed and injured so far that year,
        split across whether they were a pedestrian, passenger, driver, pillion
        passenger, pedal cyclist, or motorcyclist.
        You can see a snippet example of this data in
        <xref ref="table-ntsa-daily-report"/>.
      </p>

