      </table>

      <p>
        The second is the fatal accident report, and this one comes in two data
        sets.
        One is at the crash level, where each row represents a single incident.
        This data has columns on the location, road, vehicles involved, and a
        short description of what happened (see
        <xref ref="table-ntsa-fatal-report"/> ).
        The other data set is at the victim-level, and with one row for each
        individual involved in the incident.
        The data has columns on the individuals name, gender, age, a cause code,
        and their position (e.g., whether they were a driver, a pedestrian,
        etc).
      </p>

      <p>
        Even in this small snippet, the NTSA data has a multilevel structure:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              The yearly data has one row per year, detailing the number of
              deaths over the year for different types of victim (passengers,
              motorcyclists, etc).
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The crash-level table has one row per incident, detailing
              information on the incident.
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              The victim-level table has one row per person, detailing
              information on the individual involved in the incident.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        A single crash can involve several victims, so the tables are linked,
        but you can't just swap one for the other.
        This is exactly the kind of multilevel data structure we talked about in
        <xref ref="ch-multilevel-data"/>.
      </p>

