      <p>
        Muguro et al.
        (2020), a team of researchers from Gifu University in Japan and Dedan
        Kimathi University of Technology in Kenya, noticed that despite the
        problem of road safety in Kenya, there was very little systematic
        research on it.
        They took on five years of NTSA data, from 2015 to 2020, to understand
        how often crashes were happening, what was causing them, and where in
        Kenya the burden was greatest.
        But before they could do any of that, they had to get to know the data
        itself: what was in it, how it was structured, and what questions it
        could actually answer.
      </p>

      <p>
        We think a really good place to start is Table 1 of their paper, which
        we've reproduced in <xref ref="table-ntsa-crash-causes"/>.
        It's a frequency table: a count of how many incidents were recorded for
        each cause of crash, sorted from most to least common.
      </p>

      <table xml:id="table-ntsa-crash-causes">
        <title>
          Leading causes of traffic crashes in Kenya, from January to October
          2019.
          Source: Muguro et al.
          (2020), reproducing NTSA data.
        </title>

        <tabular halign="right">
          <col halign="right"/>
          <col halign="left"/>
          <col halign="right"/>
          <row header="yes">
            <cell>No.</cell>
            <cell>Cause</cell>
            <cell>Incidence count</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>1</cell>
            <cell>Hit and run</cell>
            <cell>925</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>2</cell>
            <cell>Losing control</cell>
            <cell>451</cell>
          </row>

          <row>
            <cell>3</cell>
