      <p>
        Have a look at the top of the list.
        Hit-and-run was the most common cause of a crash between January and
        October 2019, and by a long way, with 925 incidents.
        That's more than double the next cause!
        Excessive speeding, which arguably gets the most attention in public and
        media conversations about road safety in Kenya, is seventh, with 120
        incidents - roughly an eighth as many as hit-and-run.
        This simple table of frequencies has challenged something a lot of
        people assume.
        That's exactly what exploratory analysis is for.
      </p>

      <p>
        The researchers also looked at the same data by time of day and by
        county, which gave them <xref ref="fig-frequency-by-county-time"/>
        (copied here from Muguro et al., 2020).
        Two things jump out.
        First, the hourly chart shows a big spike at 00:00.
        Before you start worrying about midnight driving, most of these are
        crashes where the time wasn't recorded.
        Once you set that aside, most crashes happen between 16:00 and 21:00,
        which fits with the evening rush hour.
        Second, look at the counties.
        Nairobi has the most crashes, followed by Nakuru and Kiambu.
        Some of that is just population: Nairobi is Kenya's most populous
        county, so it has the most cars.
        But the gap is big enough that it looks like something else is going on
        too, like density and road conditions in the capital.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-frequency-by-county-time">
        <caption>
          Hourly distribution of accidents with peak hours highlighted (top),
          and county distribution of accidents ranked by prevalence (bottom),
          Kenya 2015-2020.
          Note that the 00:00 spike reflects crashes with unknown recorded time.
          Original source: Muguro et al.
          (2020).
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch3_incidents.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two bar charts.
              The top chart shows the hourly distribution of road accidents
              across 24 hours, with a dashed box highlighting peak hours between
              approximately 16:00 and 21:00, and a spike at 00:00 representing
              crashes with unknown recorded time.
              The bottom chart shows accident counts by Kenyan county, ranked
              from highest to lowest prevalence, with Nairobi leading
              substantially, followed by Nakuru and Kiambu.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Each NTSA crash report also has a short free-text description of what
        happened, and Muguro et al.
        (2020) explored this too.
        They used a technique called topic modelling to find patterns in the
        language across thousands of these descriptions.
        We won't go into the details of topic modelling here, but the basic idea
        is that you give a topic model a collection of short text descriptions,
        and it looks for groups of words that tend to appear together.
        Each group is treated as a theme (or <q>topic</q> ) that keeps coming
        up.
        You decide how many topics to look for, and then you look at the results
        and ask: do these groupings make sense?
      </p>

      <p>
        You can see their results as word clouds in
        <xref ref="fig-word-clouds-lda"/>, for models with three, four, five,
        and six topics.
        With three topics, the algorithm sorts all the accident descriptions
        into three groups; with four topics, four groups; and so on.
        And even if you know nothing about the method, you can read the
        clusters!
        One topic is all about <q>hit</q>, <q>run</q>, and <q>collision</q>.
        Another is about <q>knocked</q>, <q>victim</q>, and <q>down</q>.
        A third is about <q>lost</q>, <q>control</q>, and <q>road</q>.
        The four-topic model gives the cleanest categories: hit and run, head-on
        collision, loss of control, and pedestrian knockdown.
        By six topics, things start to break up and overlap.
        So choosing how many topics to use is itself an exploratory decision,
        and you make it by asking whether the clusters are clear, distinct, and
        make sense.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-word-clouds-lda">
        <caption>
          Word clouds from topic modelling of NTSA accident brief descriptions,
          for three-, four-, five-, and six-topic models.
          Word size reflects frequency within each topic.
          Original source: Muguro et al.
          (2020), <em>Cogent Engineering</em>, 7(1), 1797981, under CC BY 4.0.
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch3_wordclouds.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Four panels showing word clouds from topic models fitted to NTSA
              accident brief text.
              Panel (a) shows a three-topic model with clusters around
              collision/hit/run, vehicle/knocked/victim/down, and
              road/vehicle/control/lost.
              Panel (b) shows a four-topic model separating these into hit/run,
              knocked/victim/down, road/vehicle/control/lost, and
              head/collision.
              Panels (c) and (d) show five- and six-topic models where
              categories begin to fragment and overlap.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The descriptions commonly involve vehicles knocking down pedestrians or
        other road users, vehicles hitting or colliding with cyclists or other
        vehicles, vehicles losing control and leaving or overturning on the
        road, and head-on collisions.
        The prominence of words such as vehicle, victim, pedestrian, cyclist,
        knocked and collision shows that many of the incidents concern
        interactions between motor vehicles and vulnerable road users.
      </p>

      <p>
        Muguro et al.
        (2020) also looked at the data by <em>who</em> was hurt, instead of by
        cause, which gave <xref ref="table-ntsa-monthly-stats"/>.
        They grouped the monthly records by type of road user, and calculated
        the average number killed and injured each month, alongside the standard
        deviation, minimum, and maximum.
        These numbers really bring home how badly vulnerable road users are
        affected.
      </p>

