      </table>

      <p>
        A simple table can reveal striking patterns and raise questions or
        hypotheses about what the data are actually measuring before we do any
        deeper analysis.
        For example, on average, 490 passengers were injured every month.
        That's nearly five times the pedestrian injury average of 103, and
        higher than any other group.
        Meanwhile, the number of recorded pedestrian injuries is surprisingly
        close to the number of deaths: on average, around 100 pedestrians were
        killed and 103 injured each month.
        This is very different from passengers, where there were around 490
        injuries for every 57 deaths.
        This may reflect the particular severity of collisions involving
        pedestrians, who have little physical protection from vehicles.
        But it may also tell us something about the data: these are reported
        road-traffic incidents, not necessarily every injury that occurred.
        Minor injuries may be less likely to result in a report.
      </p>

      <p>
        The same patterns show up in the boxplot of monthly fatalities and
        injuries in <xref ref="fig-muguro-boxplot"/>, which has one box for each
        type of road user.
        Boxplots are useful here because they show the centre and spread of the
        data, as well as potential outliers.
        The line inside each box represents the median, the halfway point of the
        distribution, while the box contains the middle 50% of the data.
        The lines extending from the box are called whiskers and show the
        remaining spread according to the convention used for the plot.
        Pedestrians have the highest number of deaths on average.
        The passenger injury box is so large that it had to be scaled down by a
        factor of four.
        Motorcyclists also show considerable variation in injuries, ranging from
        39 to 217 per month.
        This raises questions about what might be driving that variation, such
        as changes in boda-boda use, enforcement, or road conditions over time.
        There's nothing fancy about these plots, they're just a different way to
        see your data.
        They're the kind of thing you'd make in your first hour with a new
        dataset, and look how much they tell you!
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-muguro-boxplot">
        <caption>
          Monthly fatalities and injuries by road user category in Kenya,
          2015-2020.
          Passenger injuries (marked with asterisk) have been scaled by 0.25 to
          fit within the range of other categories.
          From Muguro et al.
          (2020)
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch3_boxplots.png" width="90%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Two boxplots showing monthly fatalities (top) and injuries
              (bottom) for six road user categories: Pedestrians, Passengers,
              Drivers, Pillion Passengers, Cyclists, and Motor-Cyclists.
              Pedestrians and Passengers dominate fatalities.
              Passenger injuries are scaled by 0.25.
              Motorcyclists and Pillion Passengers show wide variation in both
              panels.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        So, what did Muguro et al.
        (2020) find?
        Nairobi, Nakuru, Kiambu, Machakos, and Kakamega had the largest share of
        fatal crashes, which fits with their high population density and growing
        numbers of vehicles.
        Pedestrians, pillion passengers, and motorcyclists were dying at sharply
        increasing rates, and injuries to this group of vulnerable road users
        went up by more than 250 percent over the five years.
        Across Kenya, deaths on the roads went up by 26 percent, and injuries by
        46.5 percent, between January 2015 and January 2020.
      </p>

      <p>
        And remember, everything we've looked at so far is just the exploratory
        part of their research!
        The patterns that came out of it (rising deaths, a heavy burden on
        boda-boda riders and pedestrians, hit-and-run as the leading recorded
        cause) went straight into the paper's recommendations.
        They called for targeted enforcement against hit-and-run, for
        infrastructure and policy attention in the counties where most deaths
        happen, and for urgent action to protect vulnerable road users, whose
        injury rates had tripled in five years.
        The authors also pointed out that road crashes were estimated to cost
        Kenya between 3 and 5 percent of GDP every year.
        That one figure turns a table of counts into a real argument for
        investment.
        The NTSA had been collecting this data for years, and this is a great
        example of what becomes possible when we take the time to explore it
        properly.
      </p>

      <insight xml:id="insight-ntsa-exploration">
        <title>Exploration is often the main work</title>

        <p>
          All of this was already in the NTSA data before Muguro et al.
          (2020) came along.
          This chapter aims to give you the tools to take a dataset you've never
          seen before, and find out what it's <em>actually</em> telling you.
        </p>
      </insight>

      <p>
        You can explore a real extract of NTSA crash records from April to June
        2016.
        Either by the <c>kenya_accidents_2016</c> dataset in the R package
        <c>statsdata</c>, or by going to
        <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database">The Humanitarian Data Exchange (HDX)</url>.
        This data includes the county, road, time of crash, vehicle type, and
        road user category, and is the same kind of data Muguro et al.
        (2020) worked with.
      </p>

      <p>
        <term>Source:</term> Muguro, J.K., Sasaki, M., Matsushita, K.
        and Njeri, W.
        (2020).
        <q>Trend analysis and fatality causes in Kenyan
    roads: A review of road traffic accident data between 2015 and
    2020.</q>
        <em>Cogent Engineering</em>, 7(1), 1797981.
        <url href="https://doi.org/10.1080/23311916.2020.1797981">doi.org/10.1080/23311916.2020.1797981</url>.
        Licensed under CC BY 4.0.
      </p>

      <p>
        Dataset: Kenya Road Accidents database, openAFRICA / Humanitarian Data
        Exchange.
        Available at:
        <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database">data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database</url>.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!--  SECTION 1: the data-first hook                                   -->
  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <section xml:id="sec-ch3-stack">
    <title>STACK Question Here</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Insert the STACK (Pivot Table) question here
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-ch3-TODO">
      <title>TODO</title>

      <p>
