      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-ntsa-r-exploration">
    <title>Exploring the Kenya Road Accidents Data in R</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The Kenya Road Accidents database is publicly available from the
        Humanitarian Data Exchange, and can be downloaded from
        <url href="https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database">data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database</url>,
        or accessed in the R package ( <c>statsdata</c> ).
        It covers fatal accident reports from April to June 2016, and has 378
        rows and 13 columns.
      </p>

      <p>
        Before we write any code, it's worth thinking about what kind of data
        we've got.
        Data generally comes in two broad types.
        There's <term>categorical data</term>, which is labels, names, or
        groups, like county or victim category.
        And there's <term>numerical data</term>, which is counts, measurements,
        or continuous numbers, like age or the time of a crash.
        Each type needs different tools, and this dataset has both.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you haven't already installed the packages we're using in this
        section, run this once:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        install.packages("rpivotTable")
        install.packages("readxl")
        install.packages("dplyr")
        install.packages("ggplot2")
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Then load the packages and read in the data.
        You can type out the file path, or if you're using RStudio, you can
        click the <em>Import Dataset</em> button in the Environment panel (then
        <em>From Excel...</em> ) and find the file that way.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        library(rpivotTable)
        library(readxl)
        library(dplyr)
        library(ggplot2)
        library(statsdata)

        data(kenya_accidents_2016)
        # alternatively you can download this from
        # https://data.humdata.org/dataset/kenya-road-accidents-database
        # or the raw CSV files are available to
        # download from the package repository at
        # https://github.com/PreTeXtBooks/statsdata/tree/main/data-raw.
        </code>
      </program>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-first-look">
      <title>A first look at the data</title>

      <p>
        Before doing anything else, we like to get a feel for the data.
        How many rows and columns are there?
        What are the columns called?
        What do the first few rows look like?
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # first few rows
        head(kenya_accidents_2016)

        # dimensions: rows and columns
        dim(kenya_accidents_2016)
        #&gt; [1] 378  13

        # column names and types
        glimpse(kenya_accidents_2016)
        #&gt; Rows: 378
        #&gt; Columns: 13
        #&gt; $ `TIME 24 HOURS`          &lt;chr&gt; "630", "830", "1330", ...
        #&gt; $ `BASE/SUB BASE`          &lt;chr&gt; "KITUI", "VOI", ...
        #&gt; $ COUNTY                   &lt;chr&gt; "MAKUENI", "TAITA TAVETA", ...
        #&gt; $ ROAD                     &lt;chr&gt; "KITUI-ITHOKWE", ...
        #&gt; $ PLACE                    &lt;chr&gt; "KITUI SCHOOL", "IKANGA", ...
        #&gt; $ `BRIEF ACCIDENT DETAILS` &lt;chr&gt; "HEAD ON COLLISION", ...
        #&gt; $ GENDER                   &lt;chr&gt; "M", "M", "M", ...
        #&gt; $ AGE                      &lt;chr&gt; "26", "28", "A &amp; J", ...
        #&gt; $ `CAUSE CODE`             &lt;dbl&gt; 7, 25, 98, 29, ...
        #&gt; $ VICTIM                   &lt;chr&gt; "M/CYCLIST", "M/CYCLIST", ...
        #&gt; $ NO.                      &lt;dbl&gt; 1, 1, 1, 1, ...
        #&gt; $ `Date DD/MM/YYYY`        &lt;dttm&gt; 2016-06-25, 2016-06-25, ...
        #&gt; $ ...13                    &lt;chr&gt; "so MM/DD/YYYY is the solution ...
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        A few things jump out straight away.
        The <c>AGE</c> column has values like <c>"A &amp; J"</c> (perhaps
        meaning adult and junior) mixed in with the numbers, so R has treated
        the whole column as text.
        The <c>TIME 24 HOURS</c> column is text too, and some of the values are
        a bit ambiguous.
        And there's a mysterious 13th column, with a stray note in it!
        This is completely normal with real data.
        Spotting things like this early is exactly what exploring is for.
        We come back to cleaning data in <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>, but
        for now, we'll work with what we've got.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-pivot">
      <title>Summarising data with a pivot table</title>

      <p>
        When you've got hundreds or thousands of rows, there's no way you can
        spot patterns just by scrolling through them.
        This is where a <term>pivot table</term> comes in, and it's one of our
        favourite tools for summarising data quickly.
        A pivot table does three things.
        It <em>splits</em> your data into groups using a categorical variable,
        <em>applies</em> a calculation to each group (like a count, a sum, or an
        average), and <em>combines</em> the results into one compact summary.
        The <c>rpivotTable</c> package gives you an interactive pivot table
        right inside R: you drag variables into the rows and columns, and the
        table updates straight away.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        rpivotTable(kenya_accidents_2016)
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Drag <c>COUNTY</c> into the row area, and press the little arrow by
        "County" at the top ( <xref ref="fig-pivot-tables"/> ).
        Straight away, you'll see that Nairobi has 71 incidents in the dataset.
        That's almost one for each incident each day in the three months!
        Now move <c>COUNTY</c> to the top, and <c>Date</c> to the left (
        <xref ref="fig-pivot-tables-2"/> ).
        This gives you accidents by county and by date, and you'll see that
        Kiambu, Nakuru, and Nairobi have incidents on almost every single day.
        Switch the display from table to a heatmap, and it really stands out.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-pivot-tables">
        <caption>Pivot table of accidents by County</caption>
        <image source="images/ch3_pivot_1.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Pivot table of accidents by County.
              The table shows the number of incidents for each county, with
              Nairobi having the highest count.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-pivot-tables-2">
        <caption>Pivot table of accidents by County and Date</caption>
        <image source="images/ch3_pivot_2.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Pivot table of accidents by County and Date.
              The table shows the number of incidents for each county on each
              date, with Nairobi, Kiambu, and Nakuru having incidents on almost
              every day.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Now remove <c>Date</c> from the rows and add <c>VICTIM</c> instead (
        <xref ref="fig-pivot-tables-3"/> ).
        In Nairobi, most of the victims are pedestrians.
        But Makueni looks different with only two pedestrians, but 8
        motorcyclists, 6 passengers, and 7 drivers.
        So even in this small, three-month slice of data, we can start to see
        the same county-level patterns that Muguro et al.
        (2020) found over their five years of data.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-pivot-tables-3">
        <caption>Pivot table of accidents by County and Victim</caption>
        <image source="images/ch3_pivot_3.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Pivot table of accidents by County and Victim.
              The table shows the number of incidents for each county by victim
              type, with pedestrians being the most common victims in Nairobi,
              and Makueni having a different distribution.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Pivot tables are also brilliant for spotting data quality issues.
        You'll see <c>M/CYCLIST</c> and <c>MOTOR CYCLIST</c> listed as two
        separate categories.
        They're almost certainly the same thing, just typed in differently.
        There is more on this in <xref ref="ch-cleaning-recoding"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-categorical">
      <title>Visualising data</title>

      <p>
        Categorical data tells you which group an observation belongs to.
        A good way to visualise it is a <term>bar chart</term>, where the length
        of each bar shows how many observations are in each category.
        It makes it really easy to compare groups at a glance.
      </p>

      <p>
        First, though, let's get some counts.
        If we convert our categorical columns to factors, <c>summary()</c> gives
        us a count for each level, instead of just telling us the column is
        text:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY &lt;- as.factor(kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY)
        kenya_accidents_2016$GENDER &lt;- as.factor(kenya_accidents_2016$GENDER)
        kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM &lt;- as.factor(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
        kenya_accidents_2016$ROAD &lt;- as.factor(kenya_accidents_2016$ROAD)

        summary(kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY)
        #&gt;           BARINGO           BOMET         BUNGOMA           BUSIA ELGEYO MARAKWET
        #&gt;                 6               4              10               3               2
        #&gt;              EMBU         GARISSA        HOMA BAY         KAJIADO        KAKAMEGA
        #&gt;                10               3              14              12              10
        #&gt;            KERCHO         KERICHO          KIAMBU          KILIFI       KIRINYAGA
        #&gt;                 1               5              31              18               3
        #&gt;             KISII          KISUMU           KITUI           KWALE        LAIKIPIA
        #&gt;                12               7               2               4               2
        #&gt;          MACHAKOS         MAKUENI          MAKURU        MARAKWET            MERU
        #&gt;                 9              26               1               1               5
        #&gt;            MIGORI         MOMBASA        MURANG'A         MURANGA          MWINGI
        #&gt;                 5              13               3               3               3
        #&gt;           NAIROBI          NAKURU           NANDI           NAROK       NYAHURURU
        #&gt;                71              32               1               9               1
        #&gt;           NYAMIRA       NYANDARUA           NYERI           SIAYA    TAITA TAVETA
        #&gt;                 1               1               8               4               4
        #&gt;       TRANS NZOIA     UASIN GISHU          VIHIGA            NA's
        #&gt;                 5               7               4               2

        summary(kenya_accidents_2016$ROAD)
        #&gt;         NAIROBI MOMBASA          MOMBASA NAIROBI       THIKA SUPERHIGHWAY
        #&gt;                      14                       10                        8
        #&gt;         MOMBASA-NAIROBI                  MOMBASA                 KANGUNDO
        #&gt;                       7                        6                        5
        #&gt;          NAKURU ELDORET           NAKURU NAIROBI           ELDORET NAKURU
        #&gt;                       5                        5                        4
        #&gt;           ... and so forth!

        summary(kenya_accidents_2016$GENDER)
        #&gt;   2F &amp; M  2M &amp; 2F  2M &amp; 3F    2M, F 4F &amp; INF        F        M    M &amp; F
        #&gt;        1        1        1        1        1       51      321        1
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Nairobi has the most incidents by far, followed by Nakuru and Kiambu.
        That's the same pattern Muguro et al.
        (2020) found across five whole years!
        Looking at roads, the Nairobi-Mombasa road comes up the most, but look
        closely at how it's been recorded.
        <c>NAIROBI MOMBASA</c>, <c>MOMBASA NAIROBI</c>, and
        <c>MOMBASA-NAIROBI</c> all appear as separate roads, and some of the
        <c>MOMBASA</c> entries might be the same road too.
        Just like <c>M/CYCLIST</c> and <c>MOTOR CYCLIST</c>, this is a data
        quality issue.
        If we took these counts at face value, we'd underestimate how many
        incidents happened on that road.
        Male victims outnumber female victims by more than six to one.
        And what about entries like <c>"M &amp; F"</c> and <c>"2M &amp; 2F"</c>?
        These are crashes with several victims of different genders, all
        recorded in a single row.
        Yet another data quality issue that we've found just by exploring!
      </p>

      <p>
        Summaries give us the counts, but plots let us see the distribution.
        A bar chart of county gives you an instant picture of where incidents
        are concentrated.
        You can make bar charts with the <c>barchart</c> function from the
        <c>lattice</c> package (which comes installed with R), or with
        <c>ggplot2</c>.
        We go into <c>ggplot2</c> in a lot more detail in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>.
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # one approach, using the lattice package
        library(lattice)
        barchart(kenya_accidents_2016$COUNTY)
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch3-bar">
        <caption>Bar chart of accidents by County</caption>
        <image source="images/ch3_bar.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Bar chart showing the number of accidents in each county.
              Nairobi has the highest number of incidents, followed by Nakuru
              and Kiambu.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        And we can do the same for victim category:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        # one approach, using the lattice package
        library(lattice)
        barchart(kenya_accidents_2016$VICTIM)
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch3-bar-victim">
        <caption>Bar chart of accidents by Victim category</caption>
        <image source="images/ch3_bar_victim.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Bar chart showing the number of accidents for each victim
              category.
              Male victims outnumber female victims by more than six to one.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        These are quick, exploratory plots.
        They're the kind you make to get a feel for what's in the data, before
        you decide what to look at more carefully.
        We get into more powerful (and prettier!) visualisations in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-ntsa-numerical">
      <title>Visualising numerical data</title>

      <p>
        Numerical data is about quantities you can measure.
        When we visualise numbers, we're less interested in counting groups, and
        more interested in how the values are <em>spread</em> out, and what
        their <em>distribution</em> looks like.
        The main tools here are histograms, which show how the values are
        distributed across their whole range, and boxplots, which give a compact
        summary of the spread and highlight any outliers.
      </p>

      <p>
        First, we need to convert the <c>AGE</c> column to numbers.
        Anything that isn't a number, like <c>"A &amp; J"</c>, will become
        <c>NA</c>, and that's fine for now:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        kenya_accidents_2016$AGE &lt;- as.numeric(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE)
        summary(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE)
        #&gt;    Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max.    NA's
        #&gt;    1.50   23.00   30.00   31.53   40.00   90.00     193
        </code>
      </program>

      <p>
        Ages range from 1.5 to 90 years, with a median of 30.
        But notice that 193 of the 378 rows don't have a usable age.
        That's over half!
        A histogram shows us the full distribution:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        hist(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE,
             main = "Age of road accident victims, Kenya 2016",
             xlab = "Age (years)",
             breaks = 30)
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch3-hist">
        <caption>
          Histogram of ages of road accident victims, Kenya 2016
        </caption>

        <image source="images/ch3_hist.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Histogram showing the distribution of ages of road accident
              victims in Kenya in 2016.
              Notice the spikes at ages that are multiples of five or ten,
              indicating age rounding.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Can you see the spikes at ages 30, 35, 40, 50, 60, and 70?
        They're all multiples of five or ten, and that's a classic sign of age
        rounding.
        The people filling in the records were writing down estimated ages,
        rather than exact ones.
        A boxplot shows the spread and any outliers more directly:
      </p>

      <program language="r">
        <code>
        boxplot(kenya_accidents_2016$AGE,
                main = "Age of road accident victims, Kenya 2016",
                ylab = "Age (years)")
        </code>
      </program>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch3-box">
        <caption>Boxplot of ages of road accident victims, Kenya 2016</caption>
        <image source="images/ch3_box.png" width="100%">
          <description>
            <p>
              Boxplot showing the distribution of ages of road accident victims
              in Kenya in 2016.
              The box covers the middle 50% of the data, with the median
              indicated by the line inside the box.
              Outliers, like the 90-year-old, are shown as individual points.
            </p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The 90-year-old shows up as a single point, sitting on its own above
        everyone else.
        The box covers the middle 50% of the data, from roughly 23 to 40 years
        old, with a median of around 30.
        So most of the people involved are aged somewhere between 20 and 40:
        working-age adults.
        Are these the boda-boda riders and pedestrians we saw in the victim
        categories?
        That's something we can explore too.
      </p>

      <p>
        Scatter plots are the standard way to look at how two numerical
        variables relate to each other.
        The Kenya accidents data doesn't really have many pairs of numerical
        variables to compare, but we use scatter plots a lot in
        <xref ref="ch-visualising-multidimensional-data"/>, on some richer
        datasets.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>
  <exercises xml:id="exercises-exploring-data">
    <title>Exercises</title>

    <exercise xml:id="ex-explore-bill-length">
      <statement>
        <p>
          For this set of exercises, we're going back to the flamingo data from
          <xref ref="ch-intro"/>.
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Before you calculate anything, make a guess.
                When you plot <c>bill_length_mm</c>, do you think it'll have one
                hump, or two?
                Now load the data, plot a histogram of <c>bill_length_mm</c>,
                and see if you were right.
                If there's more than one hump, try colouring or splitting the
                plot by <c>species</c>.
                Does that explain it?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Build a pivot table of <c>lake</c> against <c>sex</c>.
                Does the balance of males and females look different from one
                lake to another?
                Tell us which comparison you used to answer that (row
                proportions or column proportions), and why you picked it.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Now pick a categorical variable other than <c>lake</c> or
                <c>sex</c>.
                Build a pivot table of this variable against <c>species</c>.
                Do you notice any patterns?
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                Now pick a numeric variable other than <c>body_mass_g</c> or
                <c>bill_length_mm</c>.
                Look at it using a histogram, a summary table, or any other
                method you like.
                What do you notice about its distribution?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
</chapter>